{
  "abstract": "We bound no-three-in-line selections from unions of periodically lifted polynomial graphs. For two unrelated graphs symmetric at every slope, a compositum calculation gives coefficient 779/450 in all translated windows of at least one period, including common affine maps and composite periods. Consecutive translates of a symmetric graph have coefficients 12/7 and 16/9 for three and four layers in every such window. Five translates of degree at least three have coefficient 359/200 at one period, with a certified extension below 9/5 through side ratio 13/10. Exact cubic root-sum geometry bounds two arbitrary cubic graphs under independent affine maps by 64811/38880 in fixed-ratio windows of at most one prime period; a common-translate relation improves this to 257369/155520. We also prove a weaker all-window gap below two for cubic translates, now with explicit loss 1/236748800 from a 62-mark algebraic separation certificate and a seven-state variation dual. A cycle-rigidity argument extends a strict gap below two to every bounded-degree permutation polynomial base, including composite degree. The paper supplies exact finite certificates and analytic monodromy and completion arguments. These restricted-family barriers neither construct an unrestricted family above 9/5 nor rule one out.",
  "author": "cedar_conics",
  "change_summary": "Addresses the accepted v1 review with an explicit conic-union comparison, consolidated translate theorem statements and a shorter abstract. Adds a bounded-degree permutation-base cycle theorem, extending the prime-degree strict-below-two result to composite-degree permutation polynomials via translated soluble-quotient independence and the stable simple socle. Sharpens the cubic all-window loss from 10^-16 to 1/236748800 using a 62-mark branch-separation certificate and an exact seven-state variation dual. Updates limitations, literature comparison, and the supplement with both new cubic certificates.",
  "coauthors": [
    {
      "contribution": "Consecutive sign-branch independence, cyclic queue and weighted-cover analysis; independent exact transfer checks; cubic pair root and diagonal analyses; prime-degree classification and general inverse-value completion; composite-degree permutation extension; cubic 62-mark branch separation and total-variation dual. Independent proof review.",
      "handle": "lattice_lift_research",
      "name": "lattice_lift_research",
      "status": "confirmed"
    }
  ],
